<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Aughrim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1691">1691</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/williamite-war-in-ireland">Williamite War in Ireland</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Aughrim was fought on 12 July 1691 near Aughrim in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-galway">County Galway</a>, between the Jacobite army of the Marquis de St Ruth and the Williamite army under Godert de Ginkel. St Ruth was killed by a cannonball and his army broke with heavy losses, making it perhaps the bloodiest battle in Irish history. It led to the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galway">Galway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a> and the end of the Williamite War.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-aughrim.jpg" alt="Battle of Aughrim" width="320" height="162" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Wyk · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Aughrim_by_Jan_Wyk_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After their defeat at the Boyne and James II's departure for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the Jacobites held Connacht behind the line of the Shannon, and William III's siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a> failed in August 1690. Their command was split between the peace party of the viceroy Tyrconnell and the war party of Patrick Sarsfield. Louis XIV sent Charles Chalmot de Saint-Ruhe to take command; he reached Limerick on 9 May 1691 with arms and food but neither troops nor money.</p><p>Godert de Ginkel took Athlone on 30 June after a bloody siege and advanced cautiously towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galway">Galway</a>. Saint-Ruhe, hoping to redeem the loss of Athlone, chose to fight, and on the morning of 12 July Ginkel found the road blocked at Aughrim by about 20,000 men, roughly as many as his own.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Jacobite infantry held the crest of Kilcommadan Hill behind hedged enclosures and a large bog, with cavalry guarding a causeway on the left. Serious fighting began between five and six in the afternoon. Assaults under Hugh Mackay on the centre were thrown back through waist-deep water by Irish foot fighting with clubbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a>, and most observers, even those sympathetic to William, judged that the Irish infantry fought exceptionally well.</p><p>Williamite cavalry then forced the causeway on the left, where the Jacobite cavalry and dragoons under Luttrell had pulled back. Riding to meet them, Saint-Ruhe paused to direct a battery and was decapitated by a cannonball. Without him the left collapsed, and at around nine o'clock the infantry on the hill broke; most of the Jacobite dead fell in the pursuit that followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Some 7,000 men are generally agreed to have died. Many Jacobite officers were killed or taken, and prisoners of the rank and file held on Lambay Island died of disease and hunger. Irish tradition remembered the battle in Séamas Dall Mac Cuarta's words as Aughrim of the slaughter, with the dead left unburied, while Loyalist and Orange Order commemorations marked it on 12 July until the Boyne replaced it in the early 19th century. A battlefield interpretative centre opened in Aughrim village in 1991.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Aughrim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Williamite War of 1689–1691 the Protestant city of Derry withstood a siege of 105 days, William III defeated James II at the Battle of the Boyne in 1690, and the <b>Battle of Aughrim</b> in 1691 preceded the Treaty of Limerick.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Military</a></li></ul></section>
